        <title type="html"><![CDATA[Add CSS Typed Arithmetic]]></title>
        <id>https://github.com/Fyrd/caniuse/issues/7398</id>
        <link href="https://github.com/Fyrd/caniuse/issues/7398"/>
        <updated>2025-11-27T07:54:07.000Z</updated>
        <content type="html"><![CDATA[<img src="https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/2644614?v=4" width="64" height="64" alt=""/><br/><ul dir="auto">
<li>
<p dir="auto"><a href="https://mastodon.social/@holger@hellinger.wtf/115260382335808586" rel="nofollow">https://mastodon.social/@holger@hellinger.wtf/115260382335808586</a></p>
<blockquote>
<p dir="auto">CSS gets smarter: with typed arithmetic (Chrome 140+) you can finally mix units in math cleanly. No more hacks to strip units. Now calc(10em / 1px) just works. Great for typography, layout, responsive scaling. Browser understands unit types and does the checks for you.</p>
</blockquote>
</li>
<li>
<p dir="auto"><a href="https://css-tricks.com/css-typed-arithmetic/" rel="nofollow">https://css-tricks.com/css-typed-arithmetic/</a></p>
</li>
<li>
<p dir="auto">Spec, part of CSS Values and Units Module Level 4: <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/css-values-4/#calc-type-checking" rel="nofollow">https://www.w3.org/TR/css-values-4/#calc-type-checking</a></p>
</li>
<li>
<p dir="auto">Chrome:</p>
<ul dir="auto">
